<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Deben</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Deben is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, flowing about 50 kilometres past Woodbridge to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> at Felixstowe Ferry. Its valley was the heart of the early kingdom of East Anglia: the royal centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rendlesham">Rendlesham</a> and the ship burials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a> both lie beside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-deben.jpg" alt="River Deben" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Circuitsight at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woodbridge_Tide_Mill_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
